    <body>
      <sec id="article-29643.s1" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>The substantia gelatinosa of Rolando (SGR) is a specialized region of grey matter located within lamina II of the dorsal horn of the spinal cord (see <bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Spinal Cord Cross-Section Showing Substantia Gelatinosa and Nuclei). This region serves a central role in modulating nociception, temperature, and crude tactile sensation. The structure is eponymously named for Italian anatomist Luigi Rolando, who first described it in the early 19th century. The SGR exhibits a translucent appearance due to a dense network of unmyelinated fibers and abundant neuropil.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>Anatomically, the SGR extends along the entire length of the spinal cord and continues rostrally into the caudal medulla, where it becomes continuous with the spinal nucleus of the trigeminal nerve.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;The structure receives primary afferent inputs predominantly from C and A&#x003b4; fibers originating in the dorsal root ganglia (DRG), which convey nociceptive and thermal information. The region also receives descending modulatory input from supraspinal centers, including the brainstem. Projections from the SGR target adjacent regions of the dorsal horn and contribute to the anterolateral (spinothalamic) pathway, transmitting processed nociceptive signals centrally. The SGR acts as a key modulatory center in pain processing by integrating peripheral inputs with descending signals, consistent with the gate control theory of pain.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r3">[3]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29643.s2" sec-type="Structure and Function">
        <title>Structure and Function</title>
        <p>
<bold>Structure</bold>
</p>
        <p>The SGR is a crescent-shaped gelatinous structure located in the dorsal horn of the spinal cord, corresponding to Rexed lamina II. In humans, lamina II is subdivided into an outer zone (lamina IIo) and an inner zone (lamina IIi), each exhibiting distinct synaptic architecture and afferent connectivity. The outer zone predominantly receives nociceptive and thermosensory input from unmyelinated C fibers, whereas the inner zone primarily processes low-threshold mechanosensory inputs from A&#x003b2; fibers.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r4">[4]</xref> Recent studies indicate that lamina II is not a homogeneous structure but contains microcircuits specialized for nociceptive, pruriceptive, and mechanoreceptive processing.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Circuit Organization</bold>
</p>
        <p>The SGR functions as a critical modulatory relay, integrating convergent sensory inputs with descending modulatory signals to influence ascending pain pathways.&#x000a0;The role of this structure is primarily that of an interneuronal network rather than a direct conduit for 2nd-order projection neurons.</p>
        <p>Primary afferent inputs to the SGR arise from DRG fibers that terminate densely within lamina II. C fibers transmit slow, burning pain, warmth, and itch.&#x000a0;A&#x003b4; fibers convey fast, sharp pain and cold. A&#x003b2; fibers signal crude touch and mechanoreception.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>Excitatory and inhibitory input to the SGR arises from ipsilateral laminae I and III to V, contralateral lamina II, and rostrocaudal intrasegmental projections. Rostral inputs are typically excitatory, whereas caudal inputs are predominantly inhibitory.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>Dense descending projections from supraspinal centers, including the periaqueductal gray, locus coeruleus, gigantocellular reticular nucleus, nucleus raphe magnus, and periventricular gray matter, mediate both facilitatory and inhibitory control over nociceptive processing.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r8">[8]</xref> Recent optogenetic studies demonstrate that selective activation of inhibitory interneurons in lamina II suppresses mechanical allodynia, providing circuit-level validation of the gate control theory of pain.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Cell Types</bold>
</p>
        <p>The SGR contains a heterogeneous population of neurons and glial cells embedded within a dense neuropil. Four predominant neuronal morphologies have been identified. Stellate cells, comprising approximately 40% of the population, possess radially branching dendrites and receive mixed sensory input. Islet cells, comprising approximately 30% of the population, exhibit elongated dendrites oriented rostrocaudally and consist primarily of inhibitory neurons releasing &#x003b3;-aminobutyric acid (GABA). Filamentous cells, representing about 20%, feature long, thin dendrites that connect multiple laminae. Curly cells, comprising the remaining 10%, display compact, coiled dendritic arborizations and are frequently associated with specific nociceptive circuits.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r10">[10]</xref></p>
        <p>Connectivity patterns vary among these neuronal subtypes.&#x000a0;Some receive direct afferent input from DRG fibers, whereas others integrate signals intralaminarly or interlaminarly before projecting to deeper regions. In addition to morphological distinctions, lamina II neurons are classified by neurochemical phenotype. Inhibitory interneurons utilize GABA or glycine, while excitatory subpopulations rely on glutamate. Glial cells, particularly astrocytes and microglia, modulate nociceptive transmission through the release of cytokines and neuromodulators and contribute to the development and maintenance of chronic pain states.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Neurotransmitters and Neuromodulators</bold>
</p>
        <p>Primary afferents predominantly release glutamate as the main excitatory neurotransmitter. C fibers frequently corelease substance P and calcitonin gene-related peptide, enhancing nociceptive signaling.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r11">[11]</xref> The SGR is enriched in endogenous opioid peptides, including enkephalins, endorphins, and dynorphins, and their receptor systems, particularly &#x003bc;-opioid receptors, which are concentrated in lamina II.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r12">[12]</xref> Descending pathways modulate lamina II circuits via serotonin, norepinephrine, GABA, and glycine.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r13">[13]</xref> Additional modulatory influences include endocannabinoid signaling, transient receptor potential vanilloid 1 channels, and neuropeptide Y, highlighting potential targets for novel analgesics.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Outputs</bold>
</p>
        <p>Interneurons within the SGR project to ipsilateral and contralateral lamina II, laminae I and III to V, and deeper dorsal horn regions. These connections indirectly influence ascending tracts, including the spinothalamic, spinoreticular, and spinomesencephalic pathways, which transmit processed sensory information to higher centers such as the thalamus, somatosensory cortex, limbic structures, reticular formation, and periaqueductal gray.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>Functionally, lamina II operates as a dynamic hub for nociceptive modulation. The balance of excitatory and inhibitory interneuron activity determines whether afferent input undergoes suppression or amplification prior to ascending transmission. In chronic pain states, maladaptive plasticity within the SGR, including enhanced excitatory drive and diminished inhibitory tone, contributes to central sensitization and abnormal amplification of pain.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r15">[15]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r16">[16]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29643.s3" sec-type="Embryology">
        <title>Embryology</title>
        <p>The SGR originates from the dorsal portion of the neural tube, specifically the alar plate. Primary neurulation begins during the 3rd week of gestation, when the notochord induces the overlying ectoderm to form the neural plate. By the end of the 4th week, neural folds fuse to form the neural tube, which differentiates into cranial and caudal portions, giving rise to the brain and spinal cord, respectively. Within the spinal cord, the dorsal alar plate thickens to form sensory regions of the dorsal horn. Lamina II arises as part of this dorsal specialization and is populated by interneurons specified by transcription factors, such as Lbx1, Pax3, and Pax7, which direct the development of somatosensory circuits.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r19">[19]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29643.s4" sec-type="Blood Supply and Lymphatics">
        <title>Blood Supply and Lymphatics</title>
        <p>The SGR receives its blood supply primarily from the paired posterior spinal arteries, which perfuse the posterior 1/3 of the spinal cord through extensive anastomotic networks.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r20">[20]</xref> The anterior spinal artery supplies the anterior 2/3 and connects circumferentially to the posterior arteries via the arterial vasocorona. Segmental medullary arteries provide supplemental perfusion, most notably the artery of Adamkiewicz in the lower thoracic and lumbar regions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r21">[21]</xref> Lymphatic drainage within the central nervous system is minimal. However, the glymphatic system contributes to the partial clearance of metabolic waste.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r22">[22]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29643.s5" sec-type="Surgical Considerations">
        <title>Surgical Considerations</title>
        <p>The SGR lies adjacent to the dorsal root entry zone, the interface where primary afferents enter the spinal cord. Dorsal root entry zone lesioning, a microsurgical technique targeting this region, is employed to treat refractory neuropathic pain resulting from conditions such as brachial plexus avulsion. Gebreyohanes et al reported that over 80% of patients achieved at least 50% pain relief, but approximately 11% experienced new deficits, underscoring the critical role of precise anatomical targeting.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r23">[23]</xref></p>
        <p>The dense population of &#x003bc;-opioid receptors in lamina II also renders the SGR a target for intrathecal drug delivery systems. Intrathecal administration of agents such as morphine&#x000a0;exerts its effect within 1 to 2 mm of the dorsal horn surface, allowing potent modulation of nociceptive transmission, as demonstrated by De Andr&#x000e9;s et al.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r24">[24]</xref> Recent advances in neuromodulation devices have enhanced both the safety and efficacy of intrathecal drug delivery systems, reinforcing their utility in managing chronic, treatment-resistant pain.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r25">[25]</xref></p>
        <p>Spinal cord stimulation (SCS) constitutes an established neuromodulation therapy for neuropathic pain. Analgesic effects are mediated, in part, through modulation of SGR circuitry, wherein activation of large-diameter afferents engages inhibitory interneurons and attenuates transmission from nociceptive fibers. Recent clinical reviews demonstrate improved outcomes with modern SCS systems, including high-frequency and burst stimulation, which target dorsal horn plasticity while minimizing paresthesias.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r26">[26]</xref>&#x000a0;These observations highlight lamina II circuits as critical not only for basic nociceptive physiology but also for translational applications in advanced neuromodulation therapies.</p>
        <p>Preservation of vascular structures is essential during dorsal horn surgical procedures. Compromise of the posterior spinal arteries can induce focal ischemia, disrupting dorsal horn pain pathways. Therefore, surgical approaches must balance effective targeting of lamina II with protection of adjacent laminae and descending modulatory tracts.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29643.s6" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>The SGR serves as a critical site for the modulation of nociceptive signals, integrating both primary afferent input and descending supraspinal control. Clinical interventions targeting lamina II exploit these mechanisms to attenuate pain, ranging from local anesthetics and opioids to transcutaneous and implantable neuromodulation techniques.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Gate Control Theory of Pain</bold>
</p>
        <p>The gate control theory, first proposed by Melzack and Wall in 1965, is based on experimental evidence demonstrating that nociceptive transmission from primary afferent fibers undergoes dynamic modulation within the dorsal horn prior to reaching 2nd-order projection neurons. This modulatory process, termed &#x0201c;gating,&#x0201d; determines whether incoming noxious stimuli are amplified or suppressed before ascending to higher central nervous system centers.</p>
        <p>Within the SGR, small-diameter nociceptive afferents (C and A&#x003b4; fibers) and large-diameter mechanosensory afferents (A&#x003b2; fibers) converge onto inhibitory interneurons and projection neurons. In the absence of A&#x003b2; activity, such as during tissue injury, nociceptive input inhibits interneurons and &#x0201c;opens the gate&#x0201d; for pain signals to the anterolateral system. Activation of A&#x003b2; fibers, for example, through rubbing, vibration, or massage, excites inhibitory interneurons, thereby increasing presynaptic inhibition and reducing the release of substance P and glutamate, effectively &#x0201c;closing the gate.&#x0201d;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r27">[27]</xref> This mechanism accounts for the clinical observation that tactile stimulation can transiently diminish pain. Recent reviews highlight that lamina II plasticity contributes to central sensitization in chronic pain, thereby extending the original gate control framework.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r28">[28]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Descending Pain Modulation</bold>
</p>
        <p>Descending projections from supraspinal centers exert modulatory control over lamina II activity. Dense terminations include serotonergic fibers originating from the nucleus raphe magnus, noradrenergic fibers from the locus coeruleus, and GABAergic and glycinergic fibers from the brainstem. Activation of these pathways stimulates interneurons to release endogenous opioids, including enkephalins, endorphins, and dynorphins. These peptides bind &#x003bc;- and &#x003b4;-opioid receptors on presynaptic terminals, inhibit voltage-gated calcium channels, and reduce the release of excitatory neurotransmitters such as glutamate and substance P. The cumulative effect is suppression of nociceptive signaling at the 1st synapse.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Clinical and Therapeutic Implications</bold>
</p>
        <p>The SGR is a strategic therapeutic target due to&#x000a0;its role as an integration&#x000a0;site for both peripheral and descending modulation. Local anesthetics preferentially block small-diameter nociceptive fibers, thereby reducing dorsal horn excitability. Transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation engages spinal gating mechanisms to produce analgesia through preferential activation of large-diameter fibers, as demonstrated in randomized clinical trials.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r29">[29]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r30">[30]</xref></p>
        <p>Opioid analgesics, administered systemically or intrathecally, replicate endogenous opioid effects by binding presynaptic receptors within lamina II, decreasing excitatory neurotransmitter release, and suppressing pain transmission. SCS modulates SGR circuits by activating large-diameter afferents and enhancing inhibitory interneuron activity, with clinical and experimental evidence demonstrating reductions in neuropathic pain. Collectively, these interventions illustrate the SGR&#x02019;s function as a critical&#x000a0;site for pain modulation, highlighting its relevance as a target for pharmacologic, interventional, and device-based therapies in both physiological and maladaptive pain states.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Future Directions</bold>
</p>
        <p>Future research on the SGR is advancing toward high-resolution mapping of microcircuits and functional states. Single-cell transcriptomic approaches are identifying distinct neuronal and glial subpopulations within lamina II, revealing potential molecular targets for novel analgesic interventions. At the systems level, functional MRI of the spinal cord enables the capture of activity within dorsal horn subregions during nociceptive processing, providing insights into the dynamic behavior of these circuits <italic toggle="yes">in vivo</italic>.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r31">[31]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29643.r32">[32]</xref> Computational models that integrate electrophysiological and anatomical data are also being developed to predict circuit-level changes associated with chronic pain.</p>
        <p>Integration of molecular biology, systems neuroscience, and computational modeling is expected to yield a more precise understanding of SGR function. These advances may facilitate the development of individualized, mechanism-based pain therapies.</p>
      </sec>
